WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.210
Speaker 1: The Senate is soon going to vote
on the confirmation of Joe Biden's Supreme

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.210
Court pick.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:11.639
Katamari Brown Jackson, now she will be confirmed
and it will be an accomplishment for Joe Biden,

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:12.639
rightfully so.

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:14.340
Donald Trump had as an accomplishment.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.920
I mean, he picked horrible people, but he
had as an accomplishment, not only getting

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.190
a bunch of judges confirmed to lower courts,
as has Joe Biden, but Trump also had three

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.520
Supreme Court justices to his name.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.520
And they are accomplishments.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.080
I mean, let's be honest, they are disastrous
justices who have pulled the Supreme Court

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.130
more to the right than it's been in a very
long time.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:40.780
But if you're a right wing nut, you like it
and you see it as an accomplishment.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:41.920
So when?

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.560
Not if, when Judge Jackson is confirmed to
the Supreme Court, it will be an accomplishment

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.960
for President Joe Biden, and maybe he will
get more opportunities.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.720
But we are now starting to hear from Republicans
about whether they will support the confirmation.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:04.979
Now, you know, and I know and anybody who
takes the time to look knows that Judge Jackson

00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:11.870
is as qualified as any of the nine justices
currently on the court with a wider breadth

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.460
of experience than any justice on the court.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.430
The closest is Stephen Breyer, who she would
be replacing.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.890
But it still appears many Republicans, maybe
even most, are going to be voting no.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.660
We don't yet know whether Mitt Romney will
vote to confirm Judge Jackson.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.400
Susan Collins, Republican from Maine, has
said she will vote to confirm Judge Jackson,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.010
and her explanation is so simple it's she
has the experience and she has the qualifications.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.229
That's it.

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.880
And it really should be that simple.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.549
But I'm going to give you an example of the
worst of the worst.

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:45.500
Here's a clip of Republican Senator Roy Blunt.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.280
This is almost beyond parody.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.500
He was interviewed by George Stephanopoulos
on ABC yesterday, and he says that Jackson

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.990
should be confirmed and it will be a great
accomplishment that she will be confirmed.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:02.100
It will be a high point for the country, but
he's not going to support that confirmation.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.280
And when George Stephanopoulos asks, well,
why he delivers an unintelligible word salad

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.230
reminiscent of Sarah Palin, who, by the way,
we'll talk about a little bit later today.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.880
Listen to this
Speaker 2: thing, which a Judge Katju Tunji

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.330
Brown's Jackson if you made up your mind.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:23.680
Yet while I have a George and I'm going to
be with you this morning, you know, I've thought

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:31.020
initially my sense is that the president certainly
had every good intention and every every right

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.930
in the campaign to talk about putting the
first black woman on the court.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.260
I think it's time for that to happen.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.972
I was hoping that I could be part of that.

00:02:39.972 --> 00:02:41.990
I had a great conversation with her.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.239
Really, there are two criterias I said immediately.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:50.470
One is is the person qualified for the job
and two is what's her judicial philosophy?

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.470
OK.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.610
She's certainly qualified.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:57.620
I think he's got a great personality I think
will be a good colleague on the court.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.459
But the judicial philosophy is seems to be
not the philosophy of looking at what the

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:06.470
law says and the Constitution says and applying
that.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:12.020
But going through some method that allows
you to try to look at the Constitution as

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:17.520
a more flexible document, and even now,
Speaker 1: I wish that were true.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.069
It's not true.

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:26.660
And it was actually surprising to many progressives
how centrist on the questions of judicial

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:33.550
philosophy and seeing the Constitution more
as a sacred text versus a living document,

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.480
how how centrist her answers were.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.620
This is just not true.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.430
Roy Blunt is saying,
Speaker 2: Bend the law and there are cases

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.510
